<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Sanguo yanyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> is a Chinese historical novel attributed to Luo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> in the 14th century, one of the four classic novels of Chinese literature. It dramatises the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and the wars of Wei, Shu and Wu, mixing history with legend. Its portraits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fei">Zhang Fei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> shaped how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> remembers the period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Romance of the Three Kingdoms" width="330" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanguo2.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Stories about the heroes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> were the basis of entertainment from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang dynasties. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, professional oral storytellers specialised in them. The earliest written work to combine these stories was a pinghua, the Sanguozhi Pinghua, published between 1321 and 1323. The novel draws on Chen Shou's Records of the Three Kingdoms, Pei Songzhi's annotations, earlier chronicles and Liu Yiqing's A New Account of the Tales of the World, and it combines historical figures and events with fictional scenes, speeches and supernatural episodes.</p><p>Luo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a>, a playwright who lived sometime between 1315 and 1400, is the traditional author. The novel was first printed in 1522 as Sanguozhi Tongsu Yanyi, in an edition with a preface dated 1494, and it may have circulated earlier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>. The standard text most readers know is a recension by Mao Lun and his son Mao Zonggang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The narrative runs from the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and the Yellow Turban Rebellion to the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Western Jin in 280. It has 120 chapters, about 800,000 words and nearly a thousand characters, most of them historical. Its early episodes include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a>'s seizure of the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, and the oath of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fei">Zhang Fei</a> in the Peach Garden. Liu Bei visits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> three times to recruit him; the visits are documented in other sources, but the novel expanded them into a dramatic story.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-red-cliffs">Battle of Red Cliffs</a> in 208, won by the allied forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>, is followed by Liu Bei's takeover of Yi Province. Sun Quan then takes Jing Province, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a> is captured and executed. Liu Bei is defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-xiaoting">Battle of Yiling</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>. The narrative gives particular moral and political weight to Liu Bei and Shu Han.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The novel is counted among the Four Great Classic Novels of Chinese literature and is arguably the most widely read historical novel in late imperial and modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its writing belongs to the emergence of written vernacular in the Ming period. Many Chinese proverbs derive from it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> is said to have learned Chinese military and political strategy from it, and it was among the first Chinese books translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>. It has canonical status in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and has been called the national novel of Korea.</p><p>The novel also shaped religious practice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>, already honoured in a local cult near a monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>, appears in the novel as a spirit asking the monk Pujing for his head; he is still revered as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhisattva">Bodhisattva</a> in Buddhist tradition. The story has been retold in television series, manga and video games.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romance of the Three Kingdoms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The later novel <b>Romance of the Three Kingdoms</b> made Liu Bei, Guan Yu, Zhang Fei and Zhuge Liang lasting heroes.</blockquote><a href="/state/shu-han">Shu Han</a></li></ul></section>
